of this extra velocity in a very, very real way.

247
00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:19.360
So we gave ourselves two weeks to build

248
00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:22.760
to build the sort of MVP, the first iteration of this product.

249
00:14:22.760 --> 00:14:27.640
And on day one, we decided what we were going to build that week.

250
00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:31.960
And on day one we had finished by lunchtime.

251
00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:35.880
And so it was like, oh, brilliant.

252
00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:40.120
On day two, I was catching, I was catching a train to London,

253
00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:44.240
so I, I was, I was chatting to Stu and we were like deciding what we should,

254
00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:45.520
what we should build.

255
00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:48.600
And I was on the train platform and

256
00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:52.280
I got a text saying, done 11 a.m., 11 a.m.

257
00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:53.400
and I love to think about that.

258
00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:58.120
And I'm like, gosh, what could life look like if work just existed between 9 a.m.

259
00:14:58.120 --> 00:14:59.000
and 11 a.m.?

260
00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:00.720
And then I got the rest of my day free?

261
00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:02.320
Wouldn't that be fantastic?

262
00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:05.880
But I think it's a common theme that I've heard across a few of the talks

263
00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:10.880
here is that actually this this unlocking of this velocity,

264
00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:13.800
it makes us more ambitious and we have to be more ambitious.

265
00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:19.040
And so we had to relearn, like I had to completely throw out a lot of my product

266
00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.720
toolkit about ruthless prioritization, about slicing up features.

267
00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:27.120
I had to think way, way, way ahead about what

268
00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:30.560
the or the end goal of this product was and how I wanted it all to work.

269
00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:36.080
Instead of focusing on one thing, I had to think bigger. So.

270
00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.640
So that's what we did.

271
00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:40.760
We gave ourselves two weeks to test an idea.

272
00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:44.080
We tested our idea at KubeCon.

273
00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:47.040
The idea seemed to be very well received, and so we were able

274
00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:48.480
then to make an informed decision about,

275
00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.520
actually, we're going to we're going to invest more time in this product idea.

276
00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:55.960
And so from my own kind of real lived experience,

277
00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:01.600
I feel so excited about this new era of software development,

278
00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:06.560
because you can test every idea and you can you can make more informed decisions.

279
00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:06.800
Like,

280
00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:08.680
I haven't had to go and knock on the door of a VC

281
00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:10.680
and say, please give me money, because I've got a good idea.

282
00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:14.400
I've actually been able to build a thing and test that it's a good idea.

283
00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:20.120
And so when I think about team design and I think about how we make sure

284
00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:23.840
that no matter how fast we get, we always build something with building,

285
00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:26.160
we always build something that has an impact.

286
00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.240
Here are the things that I'm keeping.

287
00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:31.160
Here are the things that I'm trashing, and here are the things that I'm trying.

288
00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:33.680
So I'm keeping empowered product teams.

289
00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:36.840
No one should have to ask permission to to help their users.

290
00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.320
Absolutely.

291
00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:41.680
Fast feedback with real users is your superpower.

292
00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:44.640
You have to really understand the problem that you're solving.

293
00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:48.600
And I think when everybody is empowered to build and everyone

294
00:16:48.600 --> 00:16:52.640
is empowered to build quickly, user experience and usability

295
00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:53.960
are going to be your differentiators.

296
00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:56.120
So don't don't forget about those.

297
00:16:56.120 --> 00:17:00.840
I'm absolutely trashing the two pizza team and my six month roadmap.

298
00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:05.160
I was at a conference earlier this year and someone suggested

299
00:17:05.160 --> 00:17:09.000
that we start calling a team, which I really liked, like the teeny

300
00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:11.200
tiny tapas team, the small plates.

301
00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:12.880
I like that a lot.

302
00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:16.640
And I'm trying forward-deployed engineer model

303
00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:19.480
the empowered engineer that sits side by side with the customer.

304
00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:23.160
Love that product engineers and I think that's a very powerful one.

305
00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:28.000
If you're building dev tools, prototyping and testing your ideas quickly

306
00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:28.840
fail fast.

307
00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:30.160
If it's a bad idea.

308
00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:33.640
And then smaller teams for sure.

309
00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:36.920
Really thinking about the right ratio, developers to product people

310
00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:42.360
to keep that flow of ideas and value moving smoothly through your organization.

311
00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:45.800
My next

312
00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:49.720
anchor is that speed requires safety,

313
00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:54.240
so we can go very, very quickly and we can break things.

314
00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:57.960
But your users aren't going to thank you for that.

315
00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:02.760
And so I think the the second pillar is,

316
00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:07.400
is about how we move quickly and do it safely.

317
00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:12.120
The path to production is so, so important.

318
00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:16.760
And I think the evolution of how we engineer our pipelines

319
00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:17.920
and our past production

320
00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:21.120
and the platforms that support them are going to be differentiating.

321
00:18:21.120 --> 00:18:24.160
And if you have these capabilities already, you're going to be able

322
00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:27.320
to unlock a lot of value from agentic software development.

323
00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:31.680
But what I've seen happen in a few places is that actually

324
00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:36.680
what was a kind of smooth process from idea into production

325
00:18:36.680 --> 00:18:40.680
becomes a little bit of a car crash somewhere after the code is written,

326
00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:44.520
and before that code delivers value to users.

327
00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:49.440
And so if there's any manual steps on that path to production

328
00:18:49.440 --> 00:18:51.560
for your software, you're going to end up with a pile up,

329
00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:54.400
whether that be code reviews, whether that be your testing suite,

330
00:18:54.400 --> 00:18:56.960
you're going to end up with a bottleneck somewhere else.

331
00:18:56.960 --> 00:19:00.280
And so investing in your path to production,

332
00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:04.640
your pipelines, your automation is so important

333
00:19:04.640 --> 00:19:08.520
to actually leveraging AI for software development.

334
00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:13.480
It's a forever truth that like high confidence, requires testing.

335
00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:16.840
And if you want high confidence quickly,

336
00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:19.880
you have to automate that.

337
00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:22.360
One of the blog posts that I particularly like,

338
00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:28.160
and this was actually from last year, so things may have changed, was from AWS

339
00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:32.480
about how they had to completely rethink their path to production,

340
00:19:32.480 --> 00:19:36.640
to be able to get the extra velocity, and to get the volume of code

341
00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:40.960
that the software developers we're delivering safely into production.

342
00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:44.600
And I think if your organization hasn't started to think about that,

343
00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:48.360
started to map that path of reduction, look for those bottlenecks.

344
00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:53.080
Think about investing time in optimizing it, then this is my sort of rice to you.

345
00:19:53.080 --> 00:19:56.600
I think that problem might not might

346
00:19:56.600 --> 00:20:00.120
not be manifesting today, but I feel like it will eventually.

347
00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:03.360
And so getting your pipelines in order is super important.

348
00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:05.960
There's really good news here

349
00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:09.880
is and that is that AI is fantastic at writing tests.

350
00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:15.000
So if you have manual testing or slow testing

351
00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:19.280
on your path to production, then this is a really fantastic use case

352
00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:22.520
for using AI to improve your software development capability.

353
00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:26.280
And there's a great case study for from JP Morgan that talks about

354
00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:30.760
how they are using AI to do continuous component testing,

355
00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:35.200
making it safer to deliver a larger number of changes into production

356
00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:38.320
because you have that enhanced test coverage.

357
00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:42.920
So again, when we talk about like we talk about your team of developers today,

358
00:20:42.920 --> 00:20:47.280
you might not need all of them working on features right now.

359
00:20:47.280 --> 00:20:51.480
Today what you might want to do is split that team and have some folks

360
00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:55.040
focused on pipeline, some folks focused on

361
00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:59.080
test coverage, something that makes it safe to move quickly.

362
00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:03.560
I don't need to read out that quote.

363
00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:07.440
So yeah, in my experience,

364
00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:12.920
the cost of data is always greater than day one to scale

365
00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:18.640
and maintain an application that has real users is going to cost you

366
00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:21.400
a lot more in the long run than just getting version

367
00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:23.600
one of that application into the hands of users.

368
00:21:23.600 --> 00:21:26.240
We don't we don't talk about it enough, really.

369
00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:30.560
Users also care about this stuff.

370
00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:35.320
Like you're never going to get a feature request that comes in and says, like,

371
00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:38.440
don't be shipping any vulnerabilities, but they expect it

372
00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:40.040
like they expect it from you.

373
00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:44.240
And reliability issues will make the news,

374
00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:46.440
especially if you're on a popular platform.

375
00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:51.040
And so investing in reliability is is super important.

376
00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:56.000
We must always, always balance velocity with reliability

377
00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:00.320
because our users may not care about us shipping 100 features every month.

378
00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:01.640
What they care about

379
00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:03.080
is you actually doing the thing

380
00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:06.320
that you said you were going to do and doing it reliably and safely.

381
00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:11.120
Again, like very few

382
00:22:11.120 --> 00:22:14.680
customers will come knocking on your door and say, make sure you're secure.

383
00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:16.720
They expect it of you.

384
00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:21.600
And so good security tends to be invisible.

385
00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:24.280
It's an absence of problems.

386
00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:29.560
And what I have found is that, like good security is invisible.

387
00:22:29.640 --> 00:22:34.400
Bad security is an absolute reputation killer.

388
00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:39.000
So these are areas where we actually want to be investing time.

389
00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:43.240
And in the past, having to negotiate

390
00:22:43.240 --> 00:22:48.680
the time on the backlog to do that important work was always a trade off.

391
00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:49.600
Do we build this feature

392
00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:53.120
the users are asking for, or do we do this important invisible work?

393
00:22:54.040 --> 00:22:56.240
Now, we don't necessarily have to make those trade offs.

394
00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:59.360
We can do it all, or we should at least be trying to do it all.

395
00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:04.920
Practices like progressive delivery

396
00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:09.160
that make it safe to deliver a large,

397
00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:13.280
a large amount of change into the hands of your users are super important.

398
00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:16.040
So I'm talking about things like feature flags,

399
00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:20.600
so that you decouple feature delivery from the actual deployment of the software.

400
00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:23.360
I'm talking about blue green deployments, where you can actually have

401
00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:27.160
a new release being used by a subset of your users.

402
00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:31.160
Progressive delivery will make it much, much more safe for you

403
00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:34.840
to move quickly and deliver a lot more change to your users.

404
00:23:34.840 --> 00:23:38.640
So if you haven't got those capabilities in your platform at the moment, now

405
00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:41.680
might be the moment that now might be the time to try it out.

406
00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:46.880
I said that we always need to balance velocity and stability,

407
00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:51.120
and luckily we already have a language to reason about this.

408
00:23:51.120 --> 00:23:55.280
So I will advocate for site reliability engineering forever.

409
00:23:55.320 --> 00:24:00.160
Having SLIs — service level indicators that tell you whether your product

410
00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:03.880
is doing the right thing for its users objectives, about how often

411
00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:07.720
it has to work to meet your users needs and error budgets.

412
00:24:07.720 --> 00:24:11.600
The amount of kind of forgivable failure in in the system.

413
00:24:11.600 --> 00:24:14.760
By getting these things in place, by measuring them continuously,

414
00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:18.520
you can have a better dialog about whether or not you need

415
00:24:18.520 --> 00:24:22.800
to intentionally slow down the amount of change

416
00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:25.400
that's getting shipped to your users, so that you can make sure

417
00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:28.800
that you're always meeting their expectations on reliability.

418
00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:33.920
And so when I think about the future of organizational design,

419
00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:37.160
I'm seeing a future where actually platform engineering and SRE

420
00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:40.280
are even more important than they were,

421
00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:45.760
because they unlock the velocity of the development teams who are serving

422
00:24:45.760 --> 00:24:49.800
users, the development teams who are building building features.

423
00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:53.520
So platform teams who make it safe to move quickly

424
00:24:53.600 --> 00:24:58.680
using automation, and SRE teams that can act as internal consultants,

425
00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:03.680
helping teams invest in reliability so that you can deliver with this velocity.

426
00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:04.520
But you don't do it

427
00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:08.840
at the expense of your availability and your performance of the product.

428
00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:10.840
So I'm

429
00:25:10.840 --> 00:25:13.840
going to talk another another little bit about about BIM

430
00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:18.640
and my experience of my two person team building our first product.

431
00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:22.840
So like I said, we had the first version of the product ready in two weeks.

432
00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:25.360
So what did we do after that?

433
00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:29.960
I can honestly tell you, it looks a hell of a lot like platform engineering.

434
00:25:29.960 --> 00:25:33.720
Reliability engineering, like there was not a lot of feature development.

435
00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:38.000
It was about scalability, security, and doing and doing it right.

436
00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:41.440
So while it was cheap to actually

437
00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:47.040
build like a functioning application, what then took the time was making

438
00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:50.240
sure that application was ready to scale and that it was safe to do so.

439
00:25:50.240 --> 00:25:55.760
So this is my kind of like my own experience of where the time went.

440
00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:58.000
It wasn't in feature delivery at all.

441
00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.200
It was in making sure that that that platform

442
00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.920
was robust enough to be used by real enterprise customers.

443
00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:10.200
And so when we talk about our organization design and the humans, like,

444
00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:16.320
I can see a world where actually at the moment I find platform teams, SRE,

445
00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:19.480
team security teams, they are massively outnumbered

446
00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:22.160
by the number of developers who are focused on features.

447
00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:24.920
I can see that balance shifting.

448
00:26:24.920 --> 00:26:29.360
I can see an increased and increased importance for the platform

449
00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:32.440
and the SRE capabilities, because they are the people who

450
00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:36.200
unlock the value of those feature aligned teams.

451
00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:39.040
So what am I keeping?

452
00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:41.280
What am I trashing and what am I trying?

453
00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:44.320
I'm definitely keeping SRE practices, progressive

454
00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:45.960
delivery, and internal platforms.

455
00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:47.520
These are things that make it safe.

456
00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:53.160
I am trashing anything manual on my path to production.

457
00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:56.960
That doesn't mean we can't do manual testing, it just can't

