489
00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:56.360
I really like this quote by Joseph Russia.

490
00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:59.840
The mistake would be to treat unread code as a failure of discipline,

491
00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:02.960
rather than a signal that the discipline itself must change.

492
00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.400
And I don't again, I don't have answers here.

493
00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:10.600
I just know that asking engineers to review thousands and thousands of lines

494
00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:14.560
of AI authored code is going to create a lot of very unhappy engineers.

495
00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:18.320
And so we have to figure out another way that we can build quality

496
00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:23.520
and assurance into our process without creating unsustainable work.

497
00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:27.920
Another thing that I know for sure

498
00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:32.200
people are not going to want to do is to support a fragile service.

499
00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:36.800
An agent cannot hold the page in, and so this is one of like the fungible

500
00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:41.040
sort of things that I believe, like engineering teams need to navigate.

501
00:29:41.040 --> 00:29:47.160
And it's who is owning the service in production because on

502
00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:51.640
call is still a thing and you have to have a sustainable on-call rota.

503
00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:55.600
You can't have a single engineer being on-call all of the time.

504
00:29:55.600 --> 00:29:58.120
That's not fair. That's not right. That's not sustainable.

505
00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:01.880
And so when I think about how small we might be able to make our teams,

506
00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:04.680
this is the thing that I keep coming back to. It's like, well what?

507
00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:06.360
Like what is the most

508
00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:09.360
the smallest team that you can have a sustainable on-call rota.

509
00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:13.400
And I think it's for people for people capable of supporting that,

510
00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:17.360
that product in production, because you always have a primary

511
00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:22.440
and you have a secondary and you can't be on-call more than 50% of the time.

512
00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:25.840
I know a lot of people who wouldn't even want to be on-call 50% of the time.

513
00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:29.840
So like the minimum viable human like is going to be dictated

514
00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:32.840
by some of these roles.

515
00:30:34.840 --> 00:30:38.080
I think I said at the beginning, like when we were building,

516
00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:42.000
we absolutely like we ran out of work on day one.

517
00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.800
We ran out of work on day two.

518
00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:47.160
I think the thing that I would urge folks to challenge

519
00:30:47.160 --> 00:30:53.080
is a lot of the ceremony that we surround our development teams with

520
00:30:53.440 --> 00:30:56.960
might not be adding value, or it might not be adding value anymore.

521
00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:59.920
In some cases, it might be slowing us down.

522
00:30:59.920 --> 00:31:03.680
The sprint planning that only happens every two weeks is that the right pace of

523
00:31:03.680 --> 00:31:05.360
decision making that we need?

524
00:31:07.920 --> 00:31:09.440
Do I have any do?

525
00:31:09.440 --> 00:31:11.720
Do I have any managers in the room?

526
00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:14.680
Yes, managers.

527
00:31:14.680 --> 00:31:18.760
It's a tough time to be a manager of software engineering teams,

528
00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:21.360
because we don't really know

529
00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:24.680
what the role of software engineering is evolving into.

530
00:31:24.680 --> 00:31:28.840
But one thing that I'm going to urge you all to do is to go and watch a talk

531
00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:33.080
by Sophie Weston from Qcon last year, where she talked about the broken comb

532
00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:37.640
instead of the T shaped folks develop people who have a broad

533
00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:43.400
understanding and a few various areas of depth.

534
00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:44.640
And when you're doing career.

535
00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:49.760
Coaching with folks, think about the skills that they may need

536
00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:53.160
that were that will help them think about what I've talked about.

537
00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:53.680
It's like,

538
00:31:53.680 --> 00:31:57.720
do you have engineers who are going to lean to more towards product,

539
00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:01.080
or are you going to have engineers who are going to lean more towards platform?

540
00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:04.640
And can you help people develop those skills that we really need right now?

541
00:32:05.680 --> 00:32:07.000
I am almost out of

542
00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:11.160
time, but I think one of the things that I like to do, I like to,

543
00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:14.600
I like to, I like to imagine the team that I would build in the future.

544
00:32:14.600 --> 00:32:18.360
And the one thing that is absolutely critical for me

545
00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:21.360
right now is user empathy.

546
00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:26.360
If you hire someone like and they can't sit down with a user,

547
00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:29.560
understand their problem and design a solution that solves

548
00:32:29.560 --> 00:32:33.040
that problem, then that person is going to become a bottleneck.

549
00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:34.960
They're going to need to spoon fed requirements,

550
00:32:34.960 --> 00:32:36.680
and that has become normal in our industry.

551
00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:39.680
And I think that's a practice that needs to end.

552
00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:42.200
And so you'll be very relieved

553
00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:45.200
to hear this as my final slide.

554
00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:47.760
I've been trying to be subtle. Oh, no.

555
00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:50.600
People matter.

556
00:32:50.600 --> 00:32:54.720
Like I want to make sure that every engineer has a sustainable on-call rota.

557
00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:58.720
I want to make sure that we have a language to discuss, like

558
00:32:58.720 --> 00:33:02.760
our error budgets and our availability targets, and invest appropriately.

559
00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:06.160
And I want to do realistic career planning for the skills that we need

560
00:33:06.200 --> 00:33:09.200
today, not the skills that we needed yesterday.

561
00:33:09.280 --> 00:33:12.160
I am trashing heavy planning processes.

562
00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:17.600
Two week sprints are absolutely too long right now, and mandatory code reviews

563
00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:21.200
feels like an unsustainable practice that's going to need

564
00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:24.480
to be replaced with some other ways of assuring quality.

565
00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:30.480
I'm definitely shifting left on peer review using spec driven development,

566
00:33:30.480 --> 00:33:34.600
and I'm going to encourage all of you in this room, and especially the managers,

567
00:33:34.600 --> 00:33:38.440
to think about how we create people who are less T-shaped

568
00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:43.280
and more of a broken comb, people with a diverse area, diverse areas of depth,

569
00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:47.560
because those are the sort of people who are going to thrive in this new age.

570
00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:49.880
We are

571
00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:53.040
all on a journey and we do not know where we're heading,

572
00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:56.240
and I find it exciting, but it can also be daunting.

573
00:33:56.240 --> 00:34:01.040
And so as you leave here, hopefully I've given you some ideas

574
00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:05.840
to experiment with or to help you address some of the new challenges.

575
00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:08.920
I would just urge all of you

576
00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:12.920
to experiment with empathy because it is not an easy thing

577
00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:19.400
to change, to change your discipline so fundamentally and at such speed.

578
00:34:19.400 --> 00:34:22.000
So yes, we do need to try new things.

579
00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:25.440
We need to share our learning, but do it with people.

580
00:34:25.440 --> 00:34:27.880
Don't do it to them.

581
00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:30.200
And so

582
00:34:30.200 --> 00:34:32.040
here are the things that I'm keeping.

583
00:34:32.040 --> 00:34:35.240
No matter what, no matter how good agents become,

584
00:34:35.240 --> 00:34:38.840
I'm always going to build an organization that focuses on the user.

585
00:34:38.840 --> 00:34:40.960
Build something worth building.

586
00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:42.400
Solve a real problem.

587
00:34:42.400 --> 00:34:47.680
I'm going to be investing in ways that we can move safely with speed.

588
00:34:47.680 --> 00:34:50.320
And I'm going to always, always focus on the people

589
00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:53.960
because people matter and we need to create teams

590
00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:59.720
that are joyful to work in and careers that are worth pursuing.

591
00:34:59.920 --> 00:35:02.120
So that's me. I'm Hannah.

592
00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:05.560
Come and find me for questions over a beer.

593
00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:08.560
I will. Release you now.

594
00:35:08.680 --> 00:35:09.240
Thank you.

595
00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:10.760
Thank you for.
